# [[.Name]]

A starter Goa / Goa-AI service created by `surla init`, with a starter
evaluator suite that `surla verify` can run against it.

## What is here

- `design/` declares the `[[.Name]]` service. `ask` (POST /ask) runs the
  `helper` agent on a question. `price` (GET /prices/{item}) returns an item's
  price in cents. The agent can call `price` through its `lookup_price` tool.
  `override_prompt` (PUT /prompts/{prompt_id}) replaces the helper's system
  prompt text until the process exits; it requires the key in the
  `SURLA_LIVE_TARGET_KEY` environment variable, which Surla sets for a live
  target, and rejects every call without one.
  `design/model.go` declares the C4 architecture model.
- `gen/` is generated from `design/`. After changing `design/`, run `surla gen`
  in this directory: it runs Goa's generator for this module inside Surla's
  sandbox. Never edit `gen/`. `surla gen` does not build the whole
  application against the new `gen/`, so after a design change, update the
  hand-written code in `internal/` and `cmd/` to match it before you verify a
  commit or start a live target.
  `AGENTS_QUICKSTART.md` is generated with it and maps the agent, its tools
  and the generated packages for coding agents.
- `internal/helper` is the agent's planner and `internal/service` implements the
  service.
- `cmd/[[.Name]]` serves the API. The agent runs in memory and sends every
  model call to a goa-ai model gateway given with `-model-gateway host:port`;
  the gateway holds the model provider and its API keys.
- `eval/` is a separate Go module: the starter evaluator suite. It runs the
  `[[.Name]]` binary in Surla's sandbox and serves a scripted model (not an
  LLM) through its own model gateway. Its three scenarios are:
  - `prices_tea`: asks "How much is tea?" and checks that the agent called
    `lookup_price` and answered from that call's result;
  - `unknown_item` (tagged `holdout`): asks "How much is coffee?", which the
    catalog does not price, and checks that the agent calls `lookup_price`
    and reports that it found no price;
  - `prompt_override_reaches_model`: checks that `override_prompt` rejects a
    wrong key and that the next question's model request carries the new
    prompt text. Its `--live` mode starts the
  `[[.Name]]` for `surla serve`'s `start_live_target`, planning with the model
  `surla serve` was started with. After changing
  `eval/design/`, regenerate its code with
  `surla gen -dir eval`.

The evaluator suite lives in this repository, so anyone who can read the code
can read its scenarios. Its `holdout` tag only hides that scenario's details
in the report view `surla verify -builder-view` prints; it is not a secret
test. Keep real holdout scenarios in an evaluator repository builders cannot
read.

## Next steps

```sh
git init
git add .gitignore README.md AGENTS_QUICKSTART.md go.mod go.sum design gen internal cmd eval
git commit -m "Start [[.Name]] from surla init"
surla preview -dir .
WORK_DIR="$(mktemp -d)/work"
surla verify \
  -candidate-repo . -candidate-commit "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" -binary [[.Name]]=./cmd/[[.Name]] \
  -suite-repo . -suite-commit "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" -suite-module eval -suite-command ./cmd/[[.Name]]-evals \
  -request-revision starter -work-dir "$WORK_DIR" -report-dir reports
```

`surla verify` keeps `$WORK_DIR` after the run: it holds the clones, the
built binaries, and the evaluator's private evidence, including each build's
log and each step's output in `$WORK_DIR/evidence/`. `-report-dir reports`
receives the verification report as `reports/<digest>.json`.

## Connect a coding agent and try a live target

After the first commit, start `surla serve` from this directory with
`-project .`, a bearer token of your choice, and a model. `-project` needs Git
2.45 or newer. `-project .` reads
this starter layout from the current `HEAD` commit, pins the evaluator suite
to that commit for `start_live_target` and `verify_change`, and writes runs to
`.surla/runs` and reports to `reports/` (restart `surla serve` to trust a
later `HEAD` commit). `.gitignore` keeps `.surla/`, which also holds Change
Cards, run state and live targets, out of the repository. `-model-provider
anthropic` reads `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `-model-provider openai` reads
`OPENAI_API_KEY`, and `-model-gateway-addr` uses a goa-ai model gateway
instead. Set `MODEL_NAME` to a model your provider offers:

```sh
export SURLA_AUTH_TOKEN="choose-a-secret"
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="your-anthropic-key"
MODEL_NAME="your-model-name"
surla serve -principal you@example.com \
  -model-provider anthropic -model-name "$MODEL_NAME" \
  -project .
```

To pin a specific suite commit without following `HEAD` on restart, or to use
a separate evaluator repository, write `.surla/verification.json` and pass
`-verification-config .surla/verification.json` instead of `-project .`:

```sh
mkdir -p .surla
cat > .surla/verification.json <<EOF
{
  "runs_dir": ".surla/runs",
  "report_dir": "reports",
  "candidate_repo": ".",
  "candidate_module": ".",
  "binaries": {"[[.Name]]": "./cmd/[[.Name]]"},
  "suite_repo": ".",
  "suite_commit": "$(git rev-parse HEAD)",
  "suite_module": "eval",
  "suite_command": "./cmd/[[.Name]]-evals",
  "timeout": "30m"
}
EOF
```

Register the server with your coding agent. For Claude Code, save this as
`.mcp.json` in this directory. Claude Code fills in `${SURLA_AUTH_TOKEN}`
from its environment, so start it from a shell where that variable is
exported. `.mcp.json` holds that reference, not the token itself, and
`.gitignore` keeps it out of the repository:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "surla": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "http://127.0.0.1:8090/mcp",
      "headers": {"Authorization": "Bearer ${SURLA_AUTH_TOKEN}"}
    }
  }
}
```

Other coding agents take the same URL and header in their own configuration
files.

To start a change, ask your coding agent to call `propose_change` with your
request and this project's `design/*.go` files as `design_source`, so the
analysis considers the current design; without them, it receives no current
design. Answer the card's questions and confirm it with `confirm_intent`.
The confirmed card's `request_revision` is the value to verify and approve
the change with.

To try prompt wording on a running `[[.Name]]` before you verify it,
call `start_live_target` with a full commit ID, such as the output of
`git rev-parse HEAD`, then `draft_override` with
`prompt_id` `[[.Name]].helper.system`. A draft lasts only as long as that
target: to keep it, write the text into `internal/helper/planner.go`, commit,
and verify that commit.

`-request-revision` names the confirmed change request the report is for.
`starter` is only a local label; when a change goes through the Surla server,
use the `request_revision` its Change Card shows, so `surla approve` and
`approve_merge` can match the report to that request.
